Roles of mitosis
Mitosis serves four roles. (1) GROWTH: multicellular organisms grow by cell multiplication — a fertilised egg becomes a baby by repeated mitosis plus cell enlargement. (2) REPAIR: damaged tissues (skin, bone, liver) replace lost cells with identical copies. (3) REPLACEMENT: short-lived cells are renewed continuously — red blood cells (120-day life), gut lining (days), skin surface. (4) ASEXUAL REPRODUCTION: one parent produces genetically identical offspring fast with no mate — binary fission in bacteria, budding in yeast and Hydra, runners in strawberry plants, artificial cloning (cuttings, tissue culture). Advantage: speed and no mate needed; every offspring is proven-suited to a stable environment. Disadvantage: zero variation — one disease can wipe out a whole clone population (the Cavendish banana's crisis).

Cancer: mitosis out of control
Proto-oncogenes normally promote division and tumour-suppressor genes restrain it; mutations can jam the accelerator or cut the brakes, producing cells that divide endlessly, ignore death signals and invade tissues (malignant tumours). Carcinogens that raise mutation risk include tobacco smoke, excessive alcohol, certain viruses (HPV, Hepatitis B), UV radiation and some industrial chemicals. Treatments exploit rapid division: surgery removes, radiotherapy damages, and chemotherapy poisons dividing cells (which is why hair and gut — naturally fast-dividing — suffer side effects). Prevention (no smoking, vaccination, safe sun) beats cure — a public-health message worth spreading in your community.$wdthseed$, NULL, 25, 2, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Give four roles of mitosis with examples","Explain cancer as uncontrolled mitosis","Contrast mitosis with meiosis outcomes"],"introduction":"A cut on your finger closes in days; a broken bone knits in weeks; your gut lining renews itself constantly. All of it is mitosis — until control breaks.","key_points":["Four roles with one example each","Clones share vulnerabilities","Cancer is a cell-cycle control failure"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 10 (control of the cell cycle, cancer)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod04:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
