Rocks, bodies and embryos
(1) FOSSILS: sedimentary rock layers sequence life's history — simple cells deep and old, mammals and flowering plants shallow and young; transitional fossils (Tiktaalik: fish-to-tetrapod; Archaeopteryx: dinosaur-to-bird; Australopithecus: ape-to-human) capture change mid-flight. (2) HOMOLOGOUS STRUCTURES: the pentadactyl limb — human arm, bat wing, whale flipper, mole paw — shares bone-for-bone architecture despite wildly different jobs, proving common ancestry remodelled by selection. Contrast ANALOGOUS structures (bird wing vs insect wing): similar jobs, utterly different builds — convergent evolution, not shared ancestry. Vestigial structures (human appendix and tailbone, flightless birds' wings) are retired remnants. (3) EMBRYOS: vertebrate embryos (fish, chicken, human) all grow pharyngeal slits and tails early — ancestral blueprints revealed before specialisation.

Molecules seal the case
(4) MOLECULAR EVIDENCE: DNA and protein sequences measure relatedness precisely — humans share ~99% of DNA with chimpanzees, ~60% with bananas (all life uses the same code). Cytochrome c (a respiration protein) differs by 0 amino acids between humans and chimps but ~45 between humans and yeast — exactly as ancestry predicts. Endogenous retroviruses (ancient viral DNA lodged at IDENTICAL genome positions in related species) are shared scars no designer would duplicate. Converging lines matter because they are INDEPENDENT: fossils could theoretically mislead, but fossils + anatomy + embryos + molecules agreeing by coincidence is inconceivable. This is consilience — science's strongest argument form.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 3, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Describe fossil, anatomical, embryological and molecular evidence","Explain homologous vs analogous structures","Argue why converging evidence is powerful"],"introduction":"A detective convicts with fingerprints, witnesses, CCTV and DNA together — not one clue. Evolution stands on four independent lines of evidence that all point the same way.","key_points":["Pentadactyl limb = homology flagship","Analogous structures mislead; DNA resolves","Consilience beats any single clue"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 18 (evidence for evolution)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod06$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod06:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
